'use strict';
const {course,pythonRefs}=require('./backend-helper');
const L=[];
L.push({id:'btr-unit',comp:'m1.c1',title:'Specify observable behavior with focused assertions',before:'Python functions, collections and exceptions are assumed. No testing framework installation is required for the first example.',intro:'A university billing function must charge a nonnegative amount after a discount. A test is an executable claim about that contract, not a statement that a line was reached. Begin with inputs, expected behavior and a failure that would matter to a student.',sections:[['Arrange a case before asserting a result','Arrange establishes the relevant initial state; act invokes the operation; assert compares an observation with a justified expectation. Keep setup small enough that a failure points to one behavior. A test that merely calls a function without examining its result can pass while the application computes the wrong bill.','Choose boundary cases from the rule: no discount, exact zero after discount, and rejection of a discount larger than the price. Expected values should come from the requirement or an independent simple calculation, not from calling the same implementation again.'],['Test errors and preserve useful failure information','An exception is part of a contract when the input is invalid. Assert its type and any stable public detail that matters; avoid asserting incidental traceback line numbers. Name tests after behavior so a failing report explains the business consequence.','unittest assertions remain active independently of Python optimization settings, while bare assert statements can be removed by python -O. The demonstration uses explicit checks with a raised failure for portability; production test suites should normally use a maintained runner and clear assertions.'] ],problem:'Check discounted totals and the invalid-discount boundary with synthetic amounts.',steps:['State the amount and discount contract.','Check two successful boundaries independently.','Observe the required error for invalid input.'],code:'def total(price, discount):\n    if not 0 <= discount <= price:\n        raise ValueError("invalid discount")\n    return price - discount\nchecks = [total(100, 0) == 100, total(100, 100) == 0]\ntry:\n    total(100, 101)\nexcept ValueError:\n    checks.append(True)\nelse:\n    checks.append(False)\nif not all(checks):\n    raise AssertionError("billing contract")\nprint(len(checks), all(checks))',expect:'3 True',result:'Three contract checks pass. The example does not establish tax rounding, persistence, authorization or concurrent billing correctness.',mistakes:['Computing the expected value by invoking the same function repeats its bug.','Checking only that no exception occurred leaves returned data untested.'],takeaways:['Assert an observable requirement with an independent oracle.','Include boundary and invalid cases alongside the happy path.'],refs:['unittest'],q:[
['E','A test calls total(100, 10) but checks nothing. Which evidence is missing?','The expected result','The runner filename','A second identical call','A larger test directory','Execution alone does not establish that the returned amount satisfies its requirement.','File names do not inspect values.|Repeating an unchecked call adds no oracle.|Directory size is not correctness evidence.'],
['E','Which expected value is independent when testing subtraction of a 10-unit discount from 100?','The literal 90','Calling total(100,10) again','The current production output','The number of executed lines','A known simple result can serve as an independent oracle for this stated case.','The same implementation repeats its defect.|Current behavior may be the bug.|Coverage does not supply a numeric result.'],
['M','A billing rule permits the discount to equal the price. Which boundary belongs in the test?','Zero remaining charge','An unrelated user role','A longer invoice title','Only the default discount','Equality is an explicit boundary and must produce a zero amount under the declared rule.','Roles are a separate permission concern.|Labels do not exercise the arithmetic boundary.|Defaults miss equality.'],
['M','Invalid discounts must raise ValueError. What should a regression assert?','The required exception','Every traceback line number','Any exception of any type','Only that execution is fast','The stable exception contract distinguishes intentional validation from unrelated failures.','Line numbers change with harmless edits.|An unrelated error could pass a broad catch.|Latency does not verify validation.'],
['H','A service test uses bare assert and is run with python -O. What risk matters?','Assertions may be removed','Exceptions become impossible','Functions stop returning data','All arithmetic becomes approximate','Python optimization may remove assert statements, unlike maintained framework assertion methods.','Optimization does not remove every exception.|Normal function semantics remain.|This does not change integer arithmetic.'],
['H','A test fails after two independent calculations in one block. What change improves diagnosis?','Separate behavior cases','Remove failing assertions','Copy production output as expected','Retry until one run passes','Focused cases make the broken contract easier to identify without weakening the oracle.','Removing checks hides defects.|Copying behavior can bless the bug.|A lucky pass does not explain failure.'] ]});
L.push({id:'btr-boundaries',comp:'m1.c2',title:'Choose unit, integration and end-to-end boundaries',before:'Review focused assertions. HTTP and database terminology comes from the existing foundations courses.',intro:'An order service can calculate a total correctly while writing the wrong row or sending the wrong response. Different test boundaries answer different questions. Select the smallest boundary that can demonstrate the risk, then add a few broader checks for the seams that local functions cannot prove.',sections:[['Separate speed from the question being answered','A unit test isolates a small behavior such as a pricing rule. An integration test crosses a real component boundary such as repository code and a database. An end-to-end test follows a user-visible path through the assembled application. These are useful categories, not a guarantee that every team uses identical names.','A mocked repository can establish what a service requests from its collaborator, but cannot prove SQL constraints or transaction semantics. A browser test can establish a rendered journey, but slow and variable infrastructure can obscure the cause of failure. Avoid making every arithmetic case a full browser journey.'],['Use a risk-based portfolio','Protect important pure rules with many deterministic examples; verify adapters against isolated real dependencies; keep a smaller set of representative complete journeys. Include authorization and error behavior at the layer where they actually execute.','The fixture compares expected response fields at an HTTP-adapter-shaped function and counts repository calls. It has no server socket or database; therefore it is a contract-oriented unit demonstration, not a successful deployed integration test. Naming the boundary honestly prevents false confidence.']],problem:'Demonstrate an adapter contract while explicitly recording what was substituted.',steps:['Provide a repository-shaped local fixture.','Invoke the adapter through its public function.','Check status, projection and collaborator call count.'],code:'class Repo:\n    def __init__(self): self.calls = 0\n    def find(self, key):\n        self.calls += 1\n        return {"id": key, "state": "paid", "internal": "fixture"}\ndef response(repo, key):\n    row = repo.find(key)\n    return 200, {"id": row["id"], "state": row["state"]}\nrepo = Repo()\nstatus, body = response(repo, "order-1")\nprint(status, sorted(body), repo.calls)',expect:"200 ['id', 'state'] 1",result:'The adapter projects two fields and makes one repository call. No database dialect, HTTP middleware or browser path was exercised.',mistakes:['Calling a mocked test an end-to-end proof overstates its boundary.','Moving every case to the browser increases cost without isolating simple rule defects.'],takeaways:['Name the actual boundary under test.','Exercise real seams when their semantics are the risk.'],refs:['unittest','mock'],q:[
['E','Which test best isolates a pure discount function?','A focused unit test','A live payment transfer','A production browser checkout','A nightly public-site crawl','A local unit boundary provides fast focused evidence for the pure arithmetic rule.','A live transfer adds unnecessary external effects.|Production browsing is too broad for this rule.|A crawl does not isolate arithmetic.'],
['E','What can a fake database fail to prove?','Real constraint behavior','The service called find','An argument was supplied','A local branch was selected','A substitute does not execute the actual database engine or its constraints.','Call recording can show that interaction.|Argument recording is within fake scope.|A local branch can be observed.'],
['M','A query behaves differently on the target database than on a stub. Which evidence is needed?','Isolated engine integration','More identical stub calls','A screenshot of the query text','Only a unit coverage percentage','The real engine boundary is necessary when dialect and constraint behavior matter.','Repeated stubs retain the same gap.|Text appearance does not execute semantics.|Coverage alone does not test dialect behavior.'],
['M','Which browser case gives useful broad coverage for a student portal?','One representative full journey','Every integer arithmetic variant','Only static stylesheet downloads','A database library import','A representative assembled journey checks major user-visible integration seams.','Arithmetic variants belong at a smaller boundary.|A stylesheet download misses the journey.|Importing a library is not browser behavior.'],
['H','A fake repository returns the expected object, but the real adapter misnames a column. Why can the unit test pass?','The SQL seam was replaced','The browser guarantees SQL names','Unit tests always execute migrations','The fake validated production schema','The substitute may satisfy the assumed interface while bypassing the broken real query.','Browser existence does not validate every SQL field.|Unit scope need not run migrations.|A fixture does not automatically inspect the schema.'],
['H','A team labels a direct function call an end-to-end HTTP test. What should the review correct?','The claimed test boundary','The programming language','Only the test file extension','The required business outcome','Evidence must describe which components actually executed.','The language does not decide the boundary.|Extension changes do not add HTTP execution.|The outcome still needs testing.'] ]});
L.push({id:'btr-fixtures',comp:'m1.c3',title:'Create isolated fixtures and trustworthy test doubles',before:'Use the unit/integration distinction to decide whether a substitute is appropriate.',intro:'A hospital appointment test should start with known synthetic records, not depend on yesterday’s shared staging data. Fixtures establish that state, while doubles replace specific collaborators. Both need boundaries that make tests repeatable without hiding the behavior they are meant to verify.',sections:[['Make fixture lifetime explicit','Create only the records a test requires and release resources even if the assertion fails. A fresh temporary database can test repository behavior without real patient data. Transaction rollback can be useful, but shared connections and background workers can escape a single transaction’s scope.','Independent tests should not require a previous test to create a user. Give each test its own identifiers or clean store and avoid fixed filesystem names shared by parallel workers. Where constraints matter, use the intended isolated engine rather than assuming an in-memory substitute is equivalent.'],['Use doubles for the right uncertainty','A stub supplies a controlled response; a mock can also check interactions; a fake is a simplified implementation. Specify the relevant interface so a renamed method does not silently leave the test double ahead of production. Patch the reference used by the code under test rather than an unrelated imported name.','Do not mock away the contract being tested. For an email policy unit test, a fake sender can prevent external mail while verifying a message request. A separate integration test is needed for the provider adapter. The fixture below uses temporary storage and deterministic identifiers only.']],problem:'Prove fixture cleanup and a database constraint without using an application database.',steps:['Create a temporary SQLite database with a unique key.','Insert one synthetic appointment and attempt a duplicate.','Close the connection and let temporary storage be removed.'],code:'import sqlite3, tempfile\nfrom pathlib import Path\nwith tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as directory:\n    target = Path(directory) / "fixture.sqlite"\n    with sqlite3.connect(target) as db:\n        db.execute("CREATE TABLE appointments(id TEXT PRIMARY KEY)")\n        db.execute("INSERT INTO appointments VALUES (?)", ("demo-1",))\n        try:\n            db.execute("INSERT INTO appointments VALUES (?)", ("demo-1",))\n        except sqlite3.IntegrityError:\n            print("duplicate rejected")\n    db.close()\nprint(target.exists())',expect:'duplicate rejected\nFalse',result:'SQLite rejects the duplicate and the temporary file is removed. This does not establish PostgreSQL behavior or provider/email integration.',mistakes:['Shared mutable staging fixtures make parallel outcomes order-dependent.','A double that accepts any method can hide interface drift.'],takeaways:['Own fixture setup and cleanup within the test lifetime.','Use real isolated dependencies for semantics that substitutes cannot prove.'],refs:['unittest','mock'],q:[
['E','Which appointment data should an isolated test use?','Synthetic fixture records','A downloaded patient backup','Another test’s leftover rows','The newest production appointment','Synthetic owned fixtures avoid dependence on private or changing operational data.','Real patient records are unnecessary.|Leftovers create order dependence.|Production data changes independently.'],
['E','What does a stub primarily provide?','A controlled response','A real production database','A guaranteed full integration','A human review approval','A stub substitutes a known result for a collaborator at a stated boundary.','It is not necessarily a database.|Substitution reduces integration scope.|Test doubles cannot grant human approval.'],
['M','Two parallel tests write the same fixed file. What correction reduces interference?','Separate temporary paths','A longer shared filename','Ignoring duplicate-write failures','Relying on test order','Per-test owned paths prevent workers from changing each other’s fixtures.','A shared name remains shared.|Ignored failures hide interference.|Parallel order should not be a dependency.'],
['M','A test needs to verify a unique constraint. What should it execute?','The intended isolated engine','A fake always returning success','Only the model class name','A mocked constraint-error string','The actual engine must enforce the constraint to establish its behavior.','Always-success fakes omit the rule.|A name does not execute a constraint.|A manufactured error does not prove enforcement.'],
['H','A patched sender has no effect because the service imported it under another local name. Where should the patch apply?','Where the service looks it up','Every module in the process','Only the source filename','The SMTP server configuration','Python patching must target the reference used during the operation.','Global replacement is unnecessary and risky.|A filename alone is not the lookup location.|Changing provider settings does not patch a local reference.'],
['H','A cleanup step runs only after a successful assertion. What happens when the assertion fails?','Resources may be left behind','The runner always repairs files','The database becomes read-only','The assertion is automatically retried','Cleanup must run on failure too, using context management or appropriate fixture teardown.','Runners do not guarantee arbitrary resource repair.|Failure does not inherently change storage mode.|Automatic retries are not a cleanup mechanism.'] ]});
L.push({id:'btr-traces',comp:'m2.c1',title:'Read tracebacks as evidence of a failure path',before:'Understand Python exception types and function calls.',intro:'A customer support action fails while converting an imported quantity. The last error message is a clue, while the traceback shows the active call path. Read both before changing code, and distinguish the point where failure appeared from the upstream cause.',sections:[['Locate the operation and inspect its assumptions','A Python traceback lists frames leading to the exception; the final frame is near the operation that raised it, followed by type and message. Read outward to identify which caller supplied the value. The failure line may be correct to reject malformed data while an earlier parser violated its contract.','Capture the smallest reproducible input and the relevant version/configuration. Avoid dumping full requests or credentials while collecting evidence. Preserve chained exceptions when translating low-level failures so a useful cause is not replaced by an uninformative generic error.'],['Correlate logs without inventing causality','A request identifier links events from the same operation; timestamps and concurrency mean adjacent log lines may belong to different requests. Include safe operation and outcome fields instead of assuming chronological proximity proves a shared cause.','The example extracts a deterministic function name and exception type from a local synthetic traceback. It intentionally does not compare line numbers or print sensitive inputs. A real incident needs authorized diagnostic access and the code version that produced the stack.']],problem:'Extract stable diagnostic facts from a synthetic conversion failure.',steps:['Trigger an invalid integer conversion through two functions.','Inspect traceback frame summaries.','Print the exception category and failing function only.'],code:'import traceback\ndef parse_quantity(value):\n    return int(value)\ndef handle():\n    return parse_quantity("not-a-number")\ntry:\n    handle()\nexcept ValueError as error:\n    frames = traceback.extract_tb(error.__traceback__)\n    print(type(error).__name__, frames[-1].name)',expect:'ValueError parse_quantity',result:'The traceback identifies the failing conversion function and exception category. It does not show why an external system supplied invalid data.',mistakes:['The raising line is not automatically the root cause.','Logging the complete request can turn diagnostics into a data leak.'],takeaways:['Read the call path and the violated input assumption together.','Preserve useful causes while minimizing sensitive diagnostic content.'],refs:['traceback','logging'],q:[
['E','Which traceback information identifies the exception category?','The exception type','The request font','The log directory name','The number of source files','The exception type names the class of failure, such as ValueError.','Presentation is unrelated.|A directory does not classify the exception.|Project size does not identify the failure.'],
['E','A conversion raises in parse_quantity. What remains unknown from that line alone?','Why invalid data arrived','Which function raised','That conversion failed','The reported exception type','The immediate failure location does not automatically explain the upstream source of bad data.','The frame names the function.|The exception establishes failure.|The type is part of the diagnostic.'],
['M','Concurrent logs interleave two requests. What helps associate the correct events?','A safe correlation id','Assuming adjacent lines match','Logging every credential','Sorting only by message length','A correlation identifier relates events to a specific operation without relying on adjacency.','Concurrency invalidates that assumption.|Credentials are unnecessary sensitive data.|Length does not establish identity.'],
['M','A wrapper discards the underlying exception and raises only Error. What is lost?','Useful cause information','The entire Python runtime','Every business requirement','Only the color of the log','Preserving or recording a cause helps explain the originating failure.','The runtime still executes.|Requirements are external to exceptions.|The issue is diagnostic content.'],
['H','A regression asserts exact traceback line numbers after a harmless import is added. What makes it brittle?','Incidental source positions','The stable error contract','The business input boundary','The required exception class','Line positions can change without changing observable behavior.','Stable contracts are useful assertions.|Relevant input boundaries should be tested.|The exception class can be meaningful.'],
['H','A database timeout appears in a request stack. Which conclusion is justified immediately?','The operation exceeded its wait','The database lost every row','The user lacks all permissions','A retry is always safe','A timeout establishes a waiting failure, not data loss, authority or safe retry semantics.','Data loss needs independent evidence.|Permission failure is a different diagnosis.|The first operation may have completed.'],
['M','A support log includes raw session cookies. What should change?','Remove credential disclosure','Extend retention indefinitely','Copy them into the client error','Disable every error log','Diagnostics should preserve safe context without exposing reusable credentials.','Long retention enlarges exposure.|Client errors would spread the leak.|Useful safe logging can remain.'],
['H','The code was deployed after the failing request. What should incident analysis retain?','The failing revision identity','Only the newest source view','Only the operator’s memory','A replacement exception message','The deployed revision connects traceback positions and behavior to the code that actually ran.','New source may differ.|Memory is not reproducible evidence.|New wording cannot recover historical code.'],
['M','Which client error detail is usually safer for an unexpected internal exception?','A safe message and request id','Full environment variables','Every SQL connection setting','The private credential payload','A safe external response can link to controlled internal diagnostics through an identifier.','Environment dumps can reveal secrets.|Connection details expose internals.|Credential payloads must remain private.'] ]});
L.push({id:'btr-reproduce',comp:'m2.c2',title:'Turn an intermittent report into a deterministic case',before:'Read tracebacks and separate test boundaries before reproducing an incident.',intro:'A logistics job fails only at midnight or when records arrive in a different order. Random retries of the entire application can make the symptom disappear without explaining it. Control time, ordering and dependencies to isolate the trigger.',sections:[['Record the state that changes between runs','List candidate variables: clock, random generator, request order, persisted state, process count, locale and remote responses. Save a minimal synthetic input and the version that fails. Change one assumption at a time and record both failing and nonfailing cases.','Inject a clock or seeded random source into the relevant boundary instead of waiting for tomorrow. A fixed seed reproduces one sequence; it does not prove every random sequence is safe. A deterministic test should fail before the correction and pass afterward for the intended reason.'],['Shrink the case and distinguish flakiness from risk','Remove unrelated fields and operations while preserving failure. If a test passes alone but fails in a suite, inspect shared mutable state and resource collisions. If a test fails only in parallel, inspect synchronization and implicit ordering assumptions rather than merely serializing everything.','The fixture demonstrates an expiry equality bug using an injected integer time. It does not reproduce scheduling races; concurrency requires a controlled interleaving or appropriate multi-worker test. Do not claim a clock boundary test proves distributed correctness.']],problem:'Reproduce an expiry equality bug with no sleeping or real clock dependency.',steps:['Fix the expiry timestamp as fixture data.','Compare the old and required predicates at equality.','Check neighboring times to explain the boundary.'],code:'def old_accept(now, expires): return now <= expires\ndef accept(now, expires): return now < expires\nprint(old_accept(200, 200), accept(200, 200))\nprint([accept(t, 200) for t in (199, 200, 201)])',expect:'True False\n[True, False, False]',result:'The equality case explains the defect deterministically. No real account session or multi-process race was tested.',mistakes:['Sleeping until a time boundary creates slow unstable tests.','A fixed seed covers a reproducible sequence, not every possible sequence.'],takeaways:['Control the changing input rather than hope to observe it again.','Keep the smallest failing example as a regression.'],refs:['unittest','time'],q:[
['E','What makes an expiry boundary test deterministic?','An injected fixture clock','Waiting until local midnight','Using the busiest public server','Retrying until it happens','A controlled clock makes the exact time a reproducible input.','Real time varies between runs.|Public traffic is uncontrolled.|Repetition does not isolate the trigger.'],
['E','What should a minimal reproduction retain?','The failure-triggering condition','Every unrelated data field','All production customer records','Only the final success case','The reduced case must preserve the condition that demonstrates the defect.','Unrelated fields obscure causality.|Real records are unnecessary for a synthetic reproduction.|A success alone does not show the bug.'],
['M','A test passes alone but fails after another test. What is a useful first hypothesis?','Shared mutable state','Python cannot run tests','The assertion must be removed','The user interface is too small','Order dependence often indicates leaked fixture state or resources.','Python supports isolated tests.|Removing assertions hides the defect.|Presentation size does not explain order effects.'],
['M','What does seeding a random generator establish?','One repeatable sequence','Exhaustive input coverage','Absence of every race','Identical remote responses','A seed supports reproducibility for that generator and sequence.','Other sequences remain possible.|Scheduling is separate.|Remote services are not controlled by the local seed.'],
['H','A parallel failure vanishes after adding a long sleep. What remains unresolved?','The ordering assumption','The sleep syntax','The number of test files','The existence of the test runner','A delay can hide a race without enforcing or testing the required synchronization.','Valid syntax does not establish causality.|File count does not repair ordering.|The runner is already executing.'],
['H','A retry passes after a timeout but no state is captured. What evidence was gained about the cause?','Little causal evidence','Proof the bug is fixed','Proof retries are harmless','Proof no operation completed','A later success does not identify why the earlier attempt failed or whether it had effects.','No correction was demonstrated.|Retry safety needs a contract.|Timeouts can occur after completion.'],
['M','A defect appears only with one locale’s decimal format. What should the regression control?','The relevant locale/input','Only the machine hostname','Only the output text color','A larger random sample alone','The parsing assumptions and representative formatted input define this trigger.','Hostnames do not settle decimal parsing.|Color is irrelevant.|Unfocused sampling can miss the boundary.'],
['H','A local deterministic test passes after a race fix. Which extra evidence may still be needed?','Controlled concurrent execution','Another identical serial run','Only a renamed test function','A shorter stack trace','A serial case alone cannot establish behavior under interleaving workers.','Repetition keeps the same boundary.|Naming does not add concurrency.|Trace length does not test synchronization.'],
['M','Why keep a previously failing synthetic input after a correction?','To prevent recurrence','To replace every other test','To prove all future releases safe','To avoid recording the requirement','A regression preserves the concrete defect boundary for later changes.','One case does not cover all behaviors.|Future changes can introduce different defects.|The requirement remains necessary.'] ]});
L.push({id:'btr-isolate',comp:'m2.c1',title:'Distinguish symptoms, hypotheses and confirmed causes',before:'Use controlled inputs and request correlation from the preceding debugging lessons.',intro:'A retail import reports an empty result after a failed parse. An engineer can guess that the database is empty or trace how a lower-level error was translated. Effective debugging separates observation from explanation and verifies a correction against the failing contract.',sections:[['Build a causal chain with disconfirming checks','Write down what is observed, what could explain it and which smallest check distinguishes alternatives. An empty result might mean no records, a denied query, failed parsing or swallowed errors. Check each boundary’s actual return/error contract before changing storage.','A proposed root cause should predict when the bug appears and when it does not. Verify the fix with a negative case that failed before, nearby valid cases and a check that the real error is no longer silently transformed. Avoid broad changes that happen to suppress the symptom.'],['Translate errors deliberately','Public APIs need safe stable errors, while internal diagnostics need appropriate causes and correlation. Catch a specific expected exception where the application can add meaning. Catching every exception and returning an empty list makes operational failure look like valid business data.','The example contrasts that silent-empty anti-pattern with explicit local exception translation. Its message has no real customer input. It does not prescribe exposing backend exceptions to students or retaining unrestricted tracebacks.']],problem:'Preserve the difference between an empty dataset and a failed conversion.',steps:['Create an import boundary that converts a quantity.','Translate only the known conversion error.','Observe an explicit domain error instead of empty success.'],code:'class ImportFailure(Exception): pass\ndef import_quantity(raw):\n    try:\n        return int(raw)\n    except ValueError as error:\n        raise ImportFailure("quantity format") from error\ntry:\n    import_quantity("bad")\nexcept ImportFailure as error:\n    print(str(error), type(error.__cause__).__name__)\nprint(import_quantity("12"))',expect:'quantity format ValueError\n12',result:'The known failure retains its cause internally while a valid value still works. Client responses and logging access remain separate design decisions.',mistakes:['Treating every exception as an empty success hides data-quality and availability incidents.','Changing several independent layers at once makes causal validation harder.'],takeaways:['State observations and testable hypotheses separately.','Translate known failures while preserving controlled diagnostic causes.'],refs:['errors','traceback'],q:[
['E','An empty API list follows a database exception. Which distinction must remain visible?','Failure versus empty success','The client’s preferred font','Only the route spelling','The number of comments','An unavailable query and a valid empty result have different meanings.','Presentation does not describe query success.|Route spelling is not the distinction.|Comment count is irrelevant.'],
['E','Which statement is an observation rather than a cause?','The request returned 503','The cache must be corrupt','The database certainly lost data','A library upgrade caused it','A reported status is directly observed; the other claims require causal evidence.','Cache corruption is a hypothesis.|Data loss needs verification.|Upgrade causation must be tested.'],
['M','What is useful about a hypothesis that predicts both failing and passing inputs?','It can be challenged','It needs no tests','It proves itself true','It avoids a regression case','A discriminating prediction can be checked against evidence and alternatives.','Predictions need testing.|A claim is not self-proving.|A concrete case remains valuable.'],
['M','Which catch boundary is most appropriate for a known parse error?','Specific conversion failure','Every error in the process','Only keyboard interrupts','All failures as empty results','A specific catch adds domain meaning without swallowing unrelated defects.','Broad catches hide unexpected problems.|Interrupts are not the parsing contract.|Empty success misrepresents failure.'],
['H','A change suppresses the error message but the wrong record is still written. Has it fixed the defect?','No, the contract still fails','Yes, logs are now clean','Yes, any green screen is enough','Only if the commit title says fixed','Removing symptoms does not correct the observable incorrect state transition.','Clean logs can hide failure.|Appearance is not data correctness.|Commit wording cannot establish behavior.'],
['H','Why preserve a chained exception in internal diagnostics?','To retain the originating cause','To publish all secrets','To force infinite retries','To make every error identical','Chaining can retain causal context while the public response remains controlled.','Sensitive content still needs controls.|Chaining does not choose retry policy.|It preserves distinctions.'],
['M','Two unrelated changes are applied before rerunning the failure. What becomes harder?','Attributing the correction','Counting source files','Opening the test runner','Selecting a code editor','Changing one causal factor at a time helps identify which assumption mattered.','File counts are unaffected by attribution.|The runner can still open.|Editor choice is separate.'],
['H','A safe public error omits a stack trace. What should authorized debugging still have?','Controlled correlated diagnostics','No internal evidence at all','Raw credentials in the response','A fabricated root-cause label','Privacy-safe internal evidence can support diagnosis without public disclosure.','No evidence impedes diagnosis.|Credentials must remain private.|Invented certainty is not useful.'],
['M','Which validation best supports a targeted bug fix?','Failing case plus nearby contracts','Only a successful deployment','Only one renamed variable','The absence of user complaints','The original failing case and neighboring behaviors test both correction and preservation.','Deployment alone does not inspect correctness.|Renaming is not evidence.|Silence is incomplete feedback.'] ]});
L.push({id:'btr-timeouts',comp:'m3.c1',title:'Bound retries with deadlines and idempotency',before:'REST API Design explains uncertain remote outcomes. This lesson applies that contract to reliability.',intro:'A payment-related dependency can finish its work while the client times out. Repeating the request may duplicate effects unless the operation has an appropriate idempotency contract. Reliability therefore needs bounded waiting and explicit meaning for retries, not an unlimited loop around every exception.',sections:[['Separate per-attempt timeout from total budget','A timeout limits waiting at a particular boundary; a deadline bounds the overall operation including queueing, attempts and delays. Allocate remaining time before starting each attempt. Include connection/read behavior and cancellation where the actual client supports it, and remember that client cancellation may not stop server work.','Retry only failures that the operation contract classifies as retryable. A validation error is not repaired by waiting, and an uncertain effect requires idempotency or reconciliation. Use exponential backoff with jitter under an upper bound; honor provider throttling guidance and avoid every layer multiplying retries.'],['Degrade deliberately rather than inventing success','A noncritical recommendation panel might return a clearly labeled unavailable state while core enrollment continues. A payment confirmation cannot safely invent success from cached guesses. Define which information may be stale and which operations must fail closed.','The fixture computes a bounded retry schedule with a total deadline, without sleeping, sending requests or claiming a real provider policy. Jitter is omitted to make arithmetic deterministic and must be considered for real simultaneous clients.']],problem:'Select attempts that fit a fixed operation budget without real delays.',steps:['Declare attempt and delay costs.','Check remaining budget before scheduling the next attempt.','Observe where the fixed deadline prevents further work.'],code:'budget = 10\nattempt_cost = 2\ndelays = [0, 1, 2, 4]\nelapsed = 0\nscheduled = []\nfor delay in delays:\n    if elapsed + delay + attempt_cost > budget:\n        break\n    elapsed += delay + attempt_cost\n    scheduled.append(elapsed)\nprint(scheduled)\nprint(elapsed <= budget)',expect:'[2, 5, 9]\nTrue',result:'Only three attempts fit the declared schedule. This arithmetic model does not establish network timeout behavior, cancellation or safe repeated effects.',mistakes:['A timeout does not prove that the remote operation had no effect.','Retries at multiple layers can amplify load during an outage.'],takeaways:['Bound total work as well as individual waits.','Require a retry and idempotency contract for effectful operations.'],refs:['time','errors'],q:[
['E','What does an operation deadline bound?','The total allowed time','Only a message length','Every future user session','Only one retry counter','A deadline includes the overall operation rather than an unbounded series of waits.','Message length is not time.|Sessions have separate lifecycles.|A counter alone does not measure elapsed time.'],
['E','A validation error rejects a malformed address. What should a retry policy normally do?','Return the stable failure','Retry forever immediately','Assume the address is fixed','Report remote success','Waiting does not repair invalid input under the same contract.','Infinite work harms reliability.|No input correction occurred.|Rejection is not success.'],
['M','A client times out after requesting a charge. What is still uncertain?','Whether the charge completed','Whether a timeout occurred','Whether the user clicked once','Whether the client waited','A response timeout can occur after the remote side completed its effect.','The timeout was observed.|One click does not resolve remote state.|The client did wait to its limit.'],
['M','What helps prevent duplicate effects across safe retries?','An idempotency contract','A different key per retry','A longer button label','Suppressing every error log','A stable operation identity and server-side handling can coordinate repeated requests.','New keys may represent new operations.|Labels do not control effects.|Hiding errors does not deduplicate.'],
['H','Three layers each allow four attempts. What must design review consider?','Retry amplification','Only the outer status code','Only the longest source file','Automatic exactly-once execution','Independent retry layers can multiply downstream requests rather than share one budget.','The final status can hide amplified work.|File length is irrelevant.|Retries do not automatically guarantee exactly once.'],
['H','Why add jitter to backoff for many clients?','To spread retry timing','To remove every timeout','To guarantee server capacity','To make validation errors pass','Randomized delay reduces synchronized retry bursts without guaranteeing recovery.','Timeouts still need bounds.|Capacity remains finite.|Invalid input remains invalid.'],
['M','Which fallback is honest for an optional recommendation panel?','Clearly unavailable content','A fabricated completed payment','An invented new enrollment','Unlabeled private stale records','A noncritical feature can degrade explicitly without claiming an effect it cannot verify.','Payment claims have stronger correctness needs.|Enrollment must reflect actual state.|Stale/private data needs a defined policy.'],
['H','A cancelled HTTP client call ends locally. What should not be assumed?','The server stopped its work','The client stopped waiting','The deadline was reached','The local cancellation ran','Local cancellation does not necessarily undo or stop remote execution.','That is the local observation.|A deadline may be the cancellation trigger.|The local action occurred.'],
['M','The next attempt cannot fit in the remaining deadline. What should the scheduler do?','Stop scheduling it','Reset elapsed time to zero','Ignore the total budget','Queue it without a limit','The overall budget must constrain new work rather than merely record overruns.','Resetting time falsifies the budget.|Ignoring it removes the bound.|Unbounded queues retain the risk.'] ]});
L.push({id:'btr-events',comp:'m3.c2',title:'Design structured events and meaningful health checks',before:'Use safe correlation and failure distinctions from the debugging module.',intro:'Operations staff need to know whether a student service is alive, ready to receive traffic and completing useful work. A single green page cannot answer every question. Structured events and narrowly defined health signals make failures understandable without exposing credentials or private student payloads.',sections:[['Log events with a stable schema','Choose an event category, safe request identifier, operation outcome and measured duration. Serialize data as structured fields so values are not mistaken for trusted event syntax. Keep passwords, tokens, full sensitive payloads and unrestricted exception dumps out of routine records.','A useful event distinguishes dependency failure from business rejection and includes enough version/context for authorized investigation. Define access, retention and sensitive identifier handling. Avoid labels with unbounded cardinality in metrics; request ids belong in logs/traces rather than a new metric series per request.'],['Distinguish liveness, readiness and user outcomes','Liveness asks whether the process can continue operating; readiness asks whether it should receive traffic under the deployment policy. A startup check can allow initialization time. Making liveness depend on every remote service can cause restart storms during a dependency incident.','A health endpoint should return minimal safe information. Neither a successful ping nor ready=true proves that enrollment writes or authorization are correct. Combine signals with functional tests and outcome/error metrics. The example models a readiness policy and an allowlisted event only; it does not inspect a deployed cluster.']],problem:'Emit a minimal event and distinguish process life from database readiness.',steps:['Prepare a synthetic failed dependency event.','Project only the fields needed for operational context.','Evaluate liveness separately from the readiness requirement.'],code:'import json\nevent = {"category": "dependency_timeout", "request_id": "req-demo", "duration_ms": 80, "token": "SYNTHETIC-PRIVATE"}\nallowed = {key: event[key] for key in ("category", "request_id", "duration_ms")}\nprocess_running, database_ready = True, False\nprint(json.dumps(allowed, sort_keys=True))\nprint("live", process_running, "ready", process_running and database_ready)',expect:'{"category": "dependency_timeout", "duration_ms": 80, "request_id": "req-demo"}\nlive True ready False',result:'The event contains no token and the process is live but not ready under the fixture policy. It is not a production probe implementation.',mistakes:['A deep dependency liveness check can trigger unnecessary restarts.','A healthy process is not proof of correct business outcomes.'],takeaways:['Use bounded safe event schemas and controlled diagnostics.','Define each probe according to the action the platform will take.'],refs:['logging','unittest'],q:[
['E','What does a readiness signal primarily guide?','Whether to route traffic','Which password to accept','How to grade every quiz','Whether all data is correct','Readiness tells the platform whether this instance should receive work under its policy.','Authentication is a separate boundary.|Scoring needs functional correctness.|Readiness does not prove all data.'],
['E','Which value should routine event logs exclude?','Raw access credentials','A safe event category','A nonsecret request id','A bounded duration value','Reusable credentials create exposure when copied into operational records.','Categories support diagnosis.|Safe ids support correlation.|Durations can measure behavior.'],
['M','A dependency outage makes every liveness probe fail and restart all replicas. What risk is increased?','Restart amplification','Guaranteed dependency repair','Automatic data recovery','Unlimited useful capacity','Restarting healthy processes can add load and churn without repairing an external dependency.','Restarts do not control the dependency.|Recovery needs its own mechanism.|Capacity is finite.'],
['M','Why serialize log events as structured fields?','To preserve field boundaries','To encrypt every value','To grant broader log access','To remove retention decisions','Structured serialization preserves data boundaries but does not replace privacy/access policy.','Serialization is not encryption.|Access still needs limits.|Retention remains necessary.'],
['H','A request id is added as a metric label for every request. What should be reviewed?','Unbounded cardinality','Only the spelling of id','Whether logs use spaces','Automatic duplicate suppression','A unique label per request can create excessive metric series.','Spelling is not the capacity issue.|Whitespace does not bound series.|Metrics do not automatically deduplicate distinct ids.'],
['H','The health route returns 200 while enrollment writes fail. Which conclusion follows?','Probe scope is insufficient','The write failure is impossible','The data is certainly intact','All user journeys passed','A probe proves only its tested policy and may omit business write behavior.','The failure can coexist with a healthy process.|Data integrity needs evidence.|A probe is not an end-to-end suite.'],
['M','Where should detailed internal diagnostics normally be available?','Controlled operational access','Every anonymous health caller','A public asset containing secrets','The student-facing token payload','Authorized controlled diagnostics can support repair without public disclosure.','Anonymous callers need minimal safe information.|Public secrets are inappropriate.|Tokens are not diagnostic stores.'],
['H','A team reports zero errors because it catches exceptions and logs success. What is wrong?','Signals misrepresent outcomes','The process must be healthy','The metrics guarantee correctness','The exception was necessarily harmless','Monitoring is useful only if categories and outcomes reflect actual behavior.','A running process can be wrong.|Metrics can encode false claims.|The exception requires a contract-based decision.'],
['M','A startup task needs time before traffic can be served. Which separate signal can represent that phase?','A startup check','A user password reset','An unrestricted debug dump','A random success response','Startup policy can distinguish initialization from a failed steady-state process.','Password recovery is unrelated.|Debug dumps expose unnecessary data.|Random responses are not meaningful checks.'] ]});
L.push({id:'btr-degrade',comp:'m3.c1',title:'Define graceful degradation and recovery evidence',before:'Use deadlines, idempotency and health-signal scope together.',intro:'A bookstore can continue accepting catalogue reads while its optional recommendations are unavailable, but it must not claim a stock reservation that failed. Graceful degradation is a business contract for reduced service, with recovery checks and visible limitations.',sections:[['Choose the invariant that must survive','List critical operations and acceptable reduced behavior. A stale public catalogue may be acceptable for browsing under a freshness policy; a purchase must still validate current price and stock at its authoritative boundary. A cached value is not automatically authorized or current enough for every use.','Bulkheads and concurrency limits prevent one failing feature from consuming every worker. A circuit breaker can temporarily avoid repeated calls after a defined failure threshold, but needs controlled probes and recovery transitions. It is not a replacement for timeouts, capacity planning or correctness.'],['Test transitions, not only steady success','Exercise dependency unavailable, fallback selected, recovery observed and normal behavior restored. Ensure fallback use is labeled and measurable. Keep retries bounded during recovery so returning instances are not overwhelmed by synchronized work.','The fixture selects a disclosed fallback for a noncritical feature but refuses to invent an inventory reservation. It uses local policy data, not a live cache, circuit breaker or cloud dependency. Recovery in a real system also requires concurrency and deployment-specific tests.']],problem:'Distinguish an acceptable optional-feature fallback from an unsafe critical-operation shortcut.',steps:['Define separate policies for recommendations and reservation.','Simulate a dependency outage without external calls.','Expose degraded service honestly for only the allowed feature.'],code:'def serve(feature, dependency_ok):\n    if dependency_ok:\n        return "normal"\n    if feature == "recommendations":\n        return "unavailable: optional feature"\n    return "failed: no reservation created"\nprint(serve("recommendations", False))\nprint(serve("reservation", False))\nprint(serve("reservation", True))',expect:'unavailable: optional feature\nfailed: no reservation created\nnormal',result:'The fallback policy preserves the critical reservation claim. No actual inventory change or distributed recovery was performed.',mistakes:['Calling every cached value safe ignores freshness and permission requirements.','A circuit breaker without recovery policy can become permanent failure.'],takeaways:['Define degraded behavior from business invariants.','Test failure, fallback and recovery as separate states.'],refs:['unittest','errors'],q:[
['E','Which operation should not fabricate success during an outage?','A stock reservation','An optional tip panel','A labeled unavailable widget','A static help paragraph','Critical state-changing claims must reflect authoritative outcomes.','Optional presentation may degrade.|Explicit unavailability is honest.|Static help can remain available.'],
['E','What is graceful degradation?','Defined reduced service','Reporting every failure as success','Removing every limit','Ignoring recovery forever','Degradation provides a deliberate smaller contract while preserving critical requirements.','False success hides failure.|Limits remain useful.|Recovery must be designed.'],
['M','A stale catalogue price is shown for browsing. What must purchase still do?','Validate authoritative price','Trust every cached price forever','Skip stock checks','Treat rendering as a purchase','The state-changing purchase must enforce its current authoritative pricing contract.','Freshness policy cannot be unbounded implicitly.|Stock remains relevant.|Rendering does not create an order.'],
['M','What can a bulkhead limit protect?','Capacity for other features','Every database invariant','The secrecy of all tokens','The correctness of tax rules','Separating concurrency/resources can prevent one failure domain from exhausting all workers.','Integrity needs independent enforcement.|Resource partitioning is not credential secrecy.|Business calculations still need tests.'],
['H','A circuit breaker opens after failures. What transition still requires design?','Controlled recovery probes','Unlimited immediate retries','Permanent silent success','Removal of every timeout','Recovery probes and thresholds determine when dependency calls can safely resume.','A burst can overload recovery.|An open breaker is not success.|Timeouts still bound work.'],
['H','A cached private response is used as a fallback across users. What boundary may be violated?','Identity-scoped access','Only visual alignment','Only the cache filename','The number of service methods','A fallback must preserve authorization and isolation as well as availability.','Layout does not enforce permission.|Names do not isolate content.|Method count is not the boundary.'],
['M','What should monitoring record when a fallback is used?','The degraded outcome','Only an ordinary success','The user’s raw password','Nothing until complaints arrive','Explicit degraded categories allow operators to detect reduced service and verify recovery.','Unqualified success hides the condition.|Credentials are unnecessary.|Complaints are incomplete evidence.'],
['H','A dependency recovers but the fallback persists indefinitely. Which test was likely missing?','Restoration to normal service','Another identical outage case','A larger source comment','Only the initial page render','Transition tests must verify leaving degraded state after the recovery condition is met.','Another outage test retains the same state.|Comments are not behavior evidence.|Initial render does not exercise restoration.'],
['M','Why cap work while a dependency is recovering?','To avoid a recovery surge','To force every request to fail','To erase assessment history','To disable all useful features','Bounded concurrency and retry budgets protect returning capacity from synchronized overload.','Caps need not reject every request.|History is unrelated and must remain.|Noncritical features can still operate.'] ]});
